        "text": "# Review Memo — Master Services Agreement with Brightline Cloud Services Ltd.\n\n**To:** Deal team / Business owner\n**From:** In-house counsel (Customer side)\n**Re:** Northwind–Brightline MSA, draft dated 1 Jan 2026 — material risks and recommended fixes\n\n**Overall:** The paper is broadly Customer-favorable on structure, IP, termination, and payment. The real exposure is concentrated in (1) a drafting defect that collapses the liability cap to ~$12,000, (2) a punitive late-payment clause, (3) three exhibits that the MSA relies on but that are not attached, and (4) missing/dropped sections. Do not sign until items P1-A through P1-G are closed.\n\n---\n\n## Priority 1 — Do not sign without fixing\n\n**A. §1.5 / §1.6 + §21.1 — the liability cap is effectively $12,000.**\n\"Fees\" is defined as *only* the one-time $12,000 Implementation Fee, and §21.1 caps liability at Fees paid under \"the first Order Form\" in the preceding 12 months. Combined, Vendor's total exposure for a service failure is ~$12k regardless of subscription spend. The same defect undermines §7.1 (payment obligation), §8.3 (renewal increase cap applies only to \"recurring Fees\" — arguably none), §19.4/§20.5 refunds, and §22.2 shortfalls.\n**Fix:** Redefine 1.5: *\"'Fees' means all amounts payable by Customer under Order Forms, including subscription, usage, implementation, professional services, and support fees.\"* Delete the 1.6 limitation. Amend §21.1 to: *\"…shall not exceed the greater of (i) USD [___] and (ii) the total Fees paid or payable under all Order Forms during the twelve months preceding the first event giving rise to liability.\"*\n\n**B. §7.7 — late-payment liquidated damages of 15% of annual Fees *per week*.**\nThat is >780% annualized, is a penalty rather than a genuine pre-estimate of loss, and conflicts with §7.3 (good-faith withholding, no interest on amounts not due). Even if unenforceable, it is leverage in any invoice dispute.\n**Fix:** Delete §7.7. Replace with: *\"Undisputed amounts remaining unpaid more than ten business days after written notice of non-payment accrue interest at the lesser of 1% per month and the maximum rate permitted by law. Interest does not accrue on amounts disputed in good faith under §7.3.\"*\n\n**C. Referenced exhibits are missing: SLA, security exhibit, DPA.**\n§1.7, §2.2 and §9.3 assume service levels; §2.2 and §13.4 assume a security exhibit (including Customer's testing rights); §15.2 incorporates \"the Parties' data-processing addendum.\" None is attached. Without them, §9.3's chronic-failure termination right, §19's conformance warranties for uptime, and Customer's audit/pen-test rights are illusory, and §17.1 is the only security obligation.\n**Fix:** Attach all three as conditions to signature. In the SLA: uptime commitment, credits, and chronic-failure termination without credit-as-sole-remedy language. In the DPA: subprocessor list and prior-notice/objection rights, data residency, transfer mechanism, deletion/return, audit. Also amend §2.2 precedence so the **DPA and security exhibit rank above Order Forms** — as drafted, a signed Order Form can quietly dilute security and privacy terms.\n\n**D. §15.1 — model-training loophole.**\nVendor may not train \"a generally available model\" on Customer Data — which leaves it free to train internal, private, or customer-specific models.\n**Fix:** *\"…or use Customer Data to train, fine-tune, or evaluate any machine-learning or AI model.\"* Also tighten §1.3: telemetry must be de-identified so it cannot reasonably be re-identified, with an express no-re-identification covenant.\n\n**E. §16 — missing exceptions and no compelled-disclosure protocol (16.3–16.4 appear to have been dropped).**\nThere is no standard exclusion set (public, independently developed, rightfully received) and, more importantly, no obligation on Vendor to notify Customer of a subpoena, government, or law-enforcement demand for Customer Data, to seek to redirect it to Customer, or to disclose only the minimum required.\n**Fix:** Insert both. The government-request clause is the material one given Vendor hosts the data.\n\n**F. §21.3 — Customer's own liability is uncapped, and broadly so.**\n\"Excluded Claims\" is mutual and includes breach of confidentiality/data-protection/security obligations and *all* indemnity obligations. Customer therefore has unlimited exposure for its §20.3 IP/law-violation indemnity and for any confidentiality slip. There is also no super-cap tier.\n**Fix:** Two-tier the cap: unlimited only for fraud, willful misconduct, and infringement of the other Party's IP; a super-cap (e.g., 2–3x annual Fees or a stated dollar amount) for confidentiality, data-protection, security, and indemnity claims. Ensure Vendor's super-cap is meaningfully higher than its insurance retention and consistent with §18 limits.\n\n**G. §23 \"Records and Customer Assurance\" is a heading with no text; §12 begins at 12.3; §16 skips 16.3–16.4; §4 has no Affiliate-ordering mechanics despite its heading.**\nConfirm nothing was lost in transmission, and populate §23 with what is currently absent: Customer's right to (i) receive an annual SOC 2 Type II (or equivalent) and pen-test summary, (ii) conduct or commission a security assessment/questionnaire annually and after a Security Incident, (iii) audit invoices and usage records for 24 months, and (iv) records retention. Note that §22 gives **Vendor** audit rights over Customer but Customer none over Vendor — asymmetric.\n**Also add in §4:** *\"An Order Form executed by a Customer Affiliate forms a separate agreement between Vendor and that Affiliate on these terms; Customer is not liable for that Affiliate's obligations.\"* Otherwise §2.1's Affiliate-purchase right creates ambiguous joint exposure.\n\n---\n\n## Priority 2 — Fix if you have leverage\n\n| Clause | Risk | Suggested fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| §17.2 | Breach notice only; no remediation, forensics, or cost allocation | Vendor bears investigation, containment, notification, credit-monitoring and regulator-response costs for incidents caused by Vendor; provide root-cause report within 30 days; no notice naming Customer without consent |\n| §3.1 | Subcontractors permitted with no consent, notice, or location limits | Prior written notice + Customer objection right for subprocessors touching Customer Data; flow-down of §§15–17; no offshoring/data location change without consent |\n| §18.1–18.2 | E&O at $2M is light for a hosted platform; cancellation notice only \"to the extent available\"; claims-made gap | Raise tech E&O/cyber to combined $5M+; 30 days' notice of cancellation; 2-year extended reporting period; Customer as additional insured on CGL; waiver of subrogation |\n| §9.1 | Cure period extends to 90 days for \"not reasonably curable\" breaches | Carve out payment, confidentiality, data-protection, security, and abandonment breaches from the extension |\n| §11.1–11.2 | Transition at \"rates in the expiring Order Form\" (may be none); export only on request; no insolvency protection | Require a T&M rate card in every Order Form; add continuous self-service export during the term; add standing export/escrow trigger on insolvency (§9.2 termination alone leaves data stranded) |\n| §19.3 | Malicious-code warranty limited to code \"intentionally introduced by Vendor\" | Extend to an obligation to scan with current tools and to remediate malicious code in the Services regardless of source |\n| §19.4 | Repair/replace \"within a reasonable period\" | Set an outer limit (e.g., 30 days) before refund/termination right vests |\n| §20.5 | Refund on straight-line three-year amortization | For perpetual/paid-up items under §13.2, refund should be full; confirm Customer retains §13.2 rights it has already paid for |\n| §21.2 | Silent on regulatory fines imposed on Customer | Add fines/penalties assessed against Customer arising from Vendor's breach as recoverable direct damages (or expressly within §20.2 indemnity) |\n| §29.1–29.2, §8.2, §9.4 | Dual courier + email formality; email insufficient for termination/non-renewal | Permit email with acknowledged receipt; **and** calendar the 30-day non-renewal and termination deadlines now — §8.2's reminder-based extension is helpful but do not rely on it |\n| Undefined terms | \"Authorized Users,\" \"Documentation,\" \"Service Levels,\" \"first Order Form\" used but not defined | Define, or conform to Order Form/exhibit definitions |\n| No survival clause | Post-termination effect of §§10, 11, 14, 15, 16, 20, 21, 28 unclear | Add express survival provision in §30 |\n\n---\n\n## Priority 3 — Provisions favoring us that carry their own risk\n\n- **§12.4 (Vendor non-compete).** Sits oddly inside \"Customer Responsibilities,\" is untethered to any Confidential Information use, and is broad enough to raise enforceability and competition-law questions — which also invites Vendor to demand reciprocity. Consider narrowing to: no use of Customer Confidential Information or Deliverables to develop a competing product. Business call.\n- **§7.6 (MFN).** No definition of \"similarly situated,\" no audit or true-up mechanism, so it is largely unenforceable as drafted. Either add a certification/true-up remedy (retroactive credit) or accept it as low-value.\n- **§2.3 (no click-through terms) and §9.4 (termination for convenience).** Strong for us; expect Vendor pushback and protect them in negotiation trade-offs.\n\n---\n\n## Diligence / administrative\n\n1. **Entity check:** Vendor is described as \"Brightline Cloud Services **Ltd.**, a **New York** corporation.\" Confirm exact legal name, jurisdiction, and good standing; a misnamed counterparty complicates enforcement and notice. Confirm signatory authority.\n2. **Confirm the first Order Form** contains: Fees, service levels, acceptance criteria (§6.1 depends on them), legal-notice email address (§29.1 depends on it), and transition rate card.\n3. **Confirm whether Customer Data includes regulated categories** (personal data of EU/UK/California individuals, health, financial). If so, the DPA must include the appropriate transfer mechanism and, where applicable, a BAA — none is currently contemplated.\n\nI can turn P1-A through P1-G into a redline on request."
